{
  "dom_id": "step-q237498",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/6cae669be9f8e957fd22341578ef2ae613a4293df96113a2ff0d2f91ca16697c.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the following division problem.\n\n$$\n3÷\\frac{2}{4}=\\boxed{\\,\\,}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $3$ wholes. Each whole is split into *fourths*.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/9f36fbcc4441247dc12da5e005d98f750df47fdeb3f080b3eb04f54d9dc37e7d.png)\n\n \n\n- The fourths are grouped, and each group contains $\\frac{2}{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/06d895f88f07a55bd3923f09e728e23f3338a1610d3a1dfda63240913e5a9c9b.png)\n\n \n\n- There are $6$ groups in total.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/8341c19e5be70458685bbaca3d797c018c069a38c564229b6e9d86c573feb0a9.png)\n\n \n\nSo, if we divide $3$ wholes into groups of $\\frac{2}{4},$ we get $6$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, the model represents the following division problem:\n\n$$\n3÷\\frac{2}{4}=\\boxed{6}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $6.$",
